\begin{document}

\title{A Compact Thermal Modelling Framework for Parametric Radial Multistage MEMS Thermoelectric Coolers}

\author{
	\IEEEauthorblockN{Anonymous Authors}
	\IEEEauthorblockA{Double-Blind Review \\ Anonymous Affiliation}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
	Microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) increasingly require aggressive spot cooling and thermal management solutions to mitigate performance bottlenecks. Radial multistage thermoelectric coolers (TECs) offer a promising topology to address high local heat fluxes while maintaining a compact footprint. However, the geometric complexity of radial TECs poses a significant challenge for rapid design exploration. High-fidelity 3D finite element models (FEM) are computationally expensive and poorly suited for preliminary optimisation. To bridge this gap, this paper presents a novel parametric compact thermal model (CTM) specifically developed for radial multistage MEMS TECs. The proposed framework reduces the complex 3D heat transfer problem into an equivalent 1D thermal resistance network by rigorously deriving the cross-sectional integrations of the thermoelectric elements along the radial direction. The CTM is validated against high-fidelity COMSOL Multiphysics simulations across a range of geometric and operational parameters. Furthermore, a systematic characterisation of the model's validity domain is conducted, quantifying the boundaries within which the 1D assumption holds. The results indicate that the proposed CTM offers computationally instantaneous performance evaluation while maintaining acceptable accuracy within the identified design envelope ($t_{\text{TEC}} > 200\,\mu\text{m}$, $q < 420\,\text{W/m}^2$, and wedges between 6 and 11), making it a powerful tool for the preliminary optimization of radial MEMS cooling devices.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Thermoelectric cooling, compact thermal model, MEMS, radial multistage TEC, thermal management.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
The relentless miniaturisation of microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) and advanced microelectronics has led to a significant increase in local power densities. The emergence of localised hot spots severely limits device performance, reliability, and lifespan. Consequently, dynamic spot cooling and active thermal management have become critical areas of research. Thermoelectric coolers (TECs), functioning on the Peltier effect, provide a solid-state, highly reliable, and easily integratable solution for active cooling.

Traditional planar TECs are often limited by the spatial constraints of the micro-device footprint and struggles to handle extreme concentrated heat fluxes efficiently. To overcome this, multistage planar configurations have been explored \cite{hwang_planar}. Hwang et al. demonstrated the efficacy of multistage micro-TECs using a Compact Thermal Model (CTM) that reduced computational overhead compared to finite element methods (FEM) \cite{hwang_planar}. However, traditional planar or linear geometries do not fully exploit the spatial area surrounding a concentrated circular or square hot spot.

A radial multistage TEC geometry offers a compelling alternative. In this configuration, the cooling stages expand radially outward from a central hot spot, inherently providing a larger footprint for heat rejection at the hot side while concentrating the cooling power at the centre. Despite its structural advantages, modeling radial TECs is mathematically complex due to the continuously varying cross-sectional areas of the thermoelectric (TE) legs along the radial heat flow path.

While CTMs are widely utilised in the literature for planar and linear TEC designs, there is currently no established compact modelling framework that accommodates the varying geometry and interconnected electrical topologies of radial multistage TECs. Performing design optimisation using 3D FEM (e.g., COMSOL Multiphysics) is computationally prohibitive, often requiring hours per simulation iteration.

This paper addresses this gap by proposing a parametric CTM for a novel radial multistage MEMS TEC geometry. The primary contribution of this work lies in the rigorous mathematical reduction of the 3D radial geometry into a set of equivalent 1D thermal and electrical resistances, capturing the spatial dependencies of the TE elements, electrical interconnects, and insulating layers. The model is subsequently validated against automated COMSOL Multiphysics simulations. Crucially, the validity domain of the CTM is systematically characterised to provide practitioners with quantitative boundaries for the model's application, ensuring reliable preliminary optimisation.

\section{Geometry Parametrisation}

To formulate an effective compact thermal model, the radial TEC structure must be systematically parametrised. The design comprises three primary layers stacked vertically: a central silicon heat spreader (chip layer), a thin electrical insulator layer, and the active radial TEC array layer.

The core building block of the radial TEC array is the ``wedge.'' The full $360^\circ$ circular array is divided into $N_{\text{wedges}}$ identical wedges, such that the wedge angle is $\theta = 2\pi / N_{\text{wedges}}$. As illustrated in \cref{fig:wedge_top_view}, the active cooling elements are arranged radially within each wedge. A central cylindrical heat spreader with radius $r_{\text{cyl}}$ occupies the core directly above the localized hot spot. The TEC array begins at $r_{\text{cyl}}$ and extends outward to a defined base radius, $r_{\text{base}}$.

