	\label{fig:layers_side_view}
\end{figure}

Each stage $i$ within the wedge is separated radially by a radial insulator of constant width $W_{\text{is}}$. The p-type and n-type semiconductor legs within the same stage are separated azimuthally by a gap. In this parametric framework, this gap is defined by a fill factor $f$, representing the active area proportion of the TE element.

To minimize the parameter space during optimization, the radial lengths of the consecutive stages, $L_i$, follow a geometric progression defined by a radial expansion factor $f_L$, such that $L_{i+1} = f_L \cdot L_i$. Accounting for the radial insulators, the length of the first stage $L_1$ is derived as:
\begin{equation}
	L_1 = \frac{(r_{\text{base}} - r_{\text{cyl}} - (N+1) W_{\text{is}}) (1-f_L)}{1-f_L^{N}}
\end{equation}
where $N$ is the total number of radial stages.

Electrical connectivity is established via inner interconnects (width $W_{\text{ic}}$) linking p- and n-legs of the same wedge, and outer interconnects (width $W_{\text{oc}}$) linking adjacent wedges. These are parametrized as fractional multipliers of the stage length $L_i$ and thickness $t_{\text{TEC}}$. \cref{tab:geom_params} summarizes the key geometric variables enabling the rapid construction of the geometry.

\begin{table}[H]
	\centering
	\caption{Key Geometric Parameters}
	\label{tab:geom_params}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Parameter}                 & \textbf{Description}                            \\
		\midrule
		$N$                                & Number of radial cooling stages                 \\
		$N_{\text{wedges}}$                & Number of azimuthal wedges (dictates $\theta$)  \\
		$t_{\text{TEC}}$                   & Thickness of the active TEC layer               \\
		$r_{\text{cyl}}$                   & Radius of the central heat spreader cylinder    \\
		$f_L$                              & Radial length expansion ratio                   \\
		$W_{\text{is}}$                    & Width of radial electrical insulators           \\
		$f$                                & Fill factor (azimuthal semiconductor ratio)     \\
		$f_{\text{ic,W}}, f_{\text{oc,W}}$ & Fractional widths for inner/outer interconnects \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Thermal Resistance Derivation}

To transform the 3D radial geometry into a 1D thermal network, the thermal resistance of each component is derived by integrating the radially varying cross-sectional areas. For a component bounded between inner radius $r_1$ and outer radius $r_2$, the thermal resistance is given by $R_{\text{t}} = \int_{r_1}^{r_2} \frac{dr}{\kappa \cdot A(r)}$, where $\kappa$ is the thermal conductivity and $A(r)$ is the effective cross-sectional area at radius $r$.

\subsection{Thermoelectric Legs}
The thermoelectric legs (p-type and n-type) are the active components. Due to the presence of electrical interconnects and azimuthal gaps, the cross-sectional area of a TE leg is not strictly proportional to $r \theta$. To accurately capture this geometry, the leg is subdivided into three radial regions.

To simplify the parametrization of the azimuthal gaps, a dimensionless fill factor $f$ is utilized, defined as the ratio of the active semiconductor material to the total allocated azimuthal span.

\textbf{Region I (Inner Interconnect Zone):} This region spans from $r=r_{\text{in}}$ to $r=r_{\text{in}}+W_{\text{ic}}$. The inner interconnect of thickness $t_{\text{ic}}$ and angular span $\beta_{\text{ic}}$ partially occupies the space atop the TE leg. For a single leg (spanning half the wedge angle, $\theta/2$), the effective geometric area coefficient $a_{\text{I}}$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    a_{\text{I}} = \frac{1}{2}\left( \theta \cdot t_{\text{TEC}} - \beta_{\text{ic}} \cdot t_{\text{ic}} \right)
\end{equation}
The cross-sectional area as a function of $r$ is $A_{\text{I}}(r) = f \cdot a_{\text{I}} \cdot r$. Integrating over this region yields the thermal resistance:
\begin{equation}
    R_{\text{t,TE,I}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{TE}} \cdot f \cdot a_{\text{I}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{in}} + W_{\text{ic}}}{r_{\text{in}}} \right)
\end{equation}

\textbf{Region II (Clear Zone):} This middle region spans from $r=r_{\text{in}}+W_{\text{ic}}$ to $r=r_{\text{out}}-W_{\text{oc}}$. Without any interconnect intrusion, the area coefficient is purely dictated by the leg geometry: $a_{\text{II}} = \frac{1}{2} \theta \cdot t_{\text{TEC}}$. The resulting resistance is:
\begin{equation}
    R_{\text{t,TE,II}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{TE}} \cdot f \cdot a_{\text{II}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{out}} - W_{\text{oc}}}{r_{\text{in}} + W_{\text{ic}}} \right)
\end{equation}

\textbf{Region III (Outer Interconnect Zone):} This region spans from $r=r_{\text{out}}-W_{\text{oc}}$ to $r=r_{\text{out}}$. It accounts for the outer interconnect defined by $t_{\text{oc}}$ and $\beta_{\text{oc}}$. Similar to Region I, the coefficient is $a_{\text{III}} = \frac{1}{2}(\theta \cdot t_{\text{TEC}} - \beta_{\text{oc}} \cdot t_{\text{oc}})$, yielding:
\begin{equation}
    R_{\text{t,TE,III}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{TE}} \cdot f \cdot a_{\text{III}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{out}}}{r_{\text{out}} - W_{\text{oc}}} \right)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Passive Component Resistances}
The thermal resistances of the passive layers are derived using a similar methodology, assuming radial or vertical heat flow where appropriate. \cref{tab:resistances} summarizes the final expressions for the key structural resistances within the model.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Derived Thermal and Electrical Resistances}
  \label{tab:resistances}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.15\columnwidth}p{0.8\columnwidth}@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Component} & \textbf{Equation} \\
    \midrule
    Radial Insulator & $R_{\text{is}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{is}} \theta t_{\text{TEC}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{out}} + W_{\text{is}}}{r_{\text{out}}} \right)$ \\[6pt]
    Inner Interconnect (Therm.) & $R_{\text{t,ic}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{ic}} \beta_{\text{ic}} t_{\text{ic}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{in}} + W_{\text{ic}}}{r_{\text{in}}} \right)$ \\[6pt]
    Vertical Insulator & $R_{\text{ve}} = \frac{2 t_{\text{ins}}}{\kappa_{\text{ins}} \theta (r_{\text{out}}^2 - r_{\text{in}}^2)}$ \\[6pt]
    Chip Layer (Lateral) & $R_{\text{chip}} = \frac{1}{\kappa_{\text{chip}} \theta t_{\text{chip}}} \ln \left( \frac{r_{\text{out}}}{r_{\text{in}}} \right)$ \\[6pt]
    Inner Interconnect (Elec.) & $R_{\text{e,ic}} = \frac{\rho_{\text{ic}} \cdot (r_{\text{in}} + \frac{1}{2}W_{\text{ic}}) \cdot \beta_{\text{ic}}}{W_{\text{ic}} t_{\text{ic}}}$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Network Lumping and System Formulation}
To construct the macroscopic linear network, the derived component resistances within a single stage are lumped together. As shown in \cref{fig:lumped_resistor_network}, the series resistances of regions I, II, and III for the p-type and n-type legs are combined. The parallel azimuthal thermal leakage paths (representing the gap insulators) are incorporated into an equivalent stage resistance block, which is then placed in series with the radial insulator $R_{\text{is}}$ to compute the overall stage resistance $R_{\text{TEC}}$.

\begin{figure}[H]
  \centering
  \resizebox{0.95\columnwidth}{!}{%
    \begin{circuitikz}
      \ctikzset{resistors/scale=0.5}
      % Nodes
      \node[circ, label={left:$T_{i,\text{ic}}$}](ic_1) at (0,0){};
      \node[circ, label=right:$T_{i+1,\text{ic}}$](ic_2) at (7,0){};
      \node[circ](oc) at (6,0){};

      % Top Azimuthal Insulator Path
      \draw(ic_1) to ++(0,3) to [R, l=$R_{\text{az}}/2$] ++(6,0) to (oc);
      % Top branch - Thermoelectric leg (p-type)
      \draw (0,1) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,ic}}/2$}, *-*] ++(1.5,0) to ++(0,0.5) to[R, l={ $R_{\text{t,TE-p,II}}$},*-*] ++(3,0) to ++(0,-0.5)
      to [R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,oc}}/2$},*-*] ++(1.5,0);
      \draw (0,2) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,TE-p,I}}$}, *-*] ++(1.5,0) to ++(0,-0.5);
      \draw (4.5,1.5) to ++(0,+0.5) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,TE-p,III}}$}, *-*] ++(+1.5,0) ;
      % Middle branch - Azimuthal insulator
      \draw (ic_1) to[R, l=$R_{\text{az}}$] (oc);
      % Bottom branch - Thermoelectric leg (n-type)
      \draw (0,-1) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,ic}}/2$}, *-*] ++(1.5,0) to ++(0,-0.5) to[R, l={ $R_{\text{t,TE-n,II}}$},*-*] ++(3,0) to ++(0,+0.5)
      to [R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,oc}}/2$},*-*] ++(1.5,0);
      \draw (0,-2) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,TE-n,I}}$}, *-*] ++(1.5,0) to ++(0,+0.5);
      \draw (4.5,-1.5) to ++(0,-0.5) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{t,TE-n,III}}$}, *-*] ++(+1.5,0) ;
      % Bottom Azimuthal Insulator Path
      \draw(ic_1) to ++(0,-3) to [R, l=$R_{\text{az}}/2$] ++(6,0) to (oc);
      \draw (oc) to[R, l={\footnotesize $R_{\text{is}}$}] (ic_2);
    \end{circuitikz}%
  }
  \caption{Lumped thermal resistance network for a single radial TEC stage connecting adjacent interconnect nodes.}
  \label{fig:lumped_resistor_network}
\end{figure}

By writing energy balance equations incorporating Peltier pumping ($Q_{\text{p}} = S I T_{\text{c}}$) and localised Joule heating ($\frac{1}{2} I^2 R_{\text{e}}$) for the $N$ stages across the chip and TEC layers, the system is expressed as a linear matrix equation:
\begin{equation}
   \begin{bmatrix} 
   \mathbf{A_{00}} & \mathbf{A_{0 \to \text{Si}}} & \mathbf{A_{0 \to \text{TEC}}} \\ 
   \mathbf{A_{\text{Si} \to 0}} & \mathbf{A_{\text{chip}}} & \mathbf{A_{\text{ve}}} \\ 
   \mathbf{A_{\text{TEC} \to 0}} & \mathbf{A_{\text{ve}}} & \mathbf{A_{\text{TEC}}} 
   \end{bmatrix}
   \begin{bmatrix}
    T_{0} \\ \mathbf{T_{\text{chip}}} \\ \mathbf{T_{\text{TEC}}}
   \end{bmatrix} =
   \begin{bmatrix} 
    B_0 \\ \mathbf{B_{\text{chip}}} \\ \mathbf{B_{\text{TEC}}} 
   \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

Here, $\mathbf{A_{00}}$ is the central hub scalar connecting the core cylinder. $\mathbf{A_{\text{chip}}}$ is a tridiagonal matrix governing the passive lateral spread in the silicon. $\mathbf{A_{\text{TEC}}}$ is an asymmetric tridiagonal matrix reflecting the active Peltier pumping and conduction, while the diagonal $\mathbf{A_{\text{ve}}}$ links the layers vertically. The source vector $\mathbf{B}$ contains external heat flux and stage-wise internal Joule generation. Solving this dense linear system provides the continuous radial temperature profile in a fraction of a second.

\section{Validation}

To verify the mathematical correctness and physical assumptions of the CTM, the model was validated against a full 3D Finite Element Method (FEM) simulation. A parametric 3D CAD model of a 3-stage radial TEC was created in SolidWorks and live-linked to COMSOL Multiphysics. A MATLAB API framework was developed to seamlessly update the geometric parameters, mesh the geometry, solve the 3D conjugate heat transfer and thermoelectric multiphysics equations, and extract the maximum system temperature. 

\cref{tab:validation_cases} presents five distinct validation cases encompassing different geometric constraints (thicknesses, wedge angles, length ratios) and operational boundaries (currents, heat fluxes). 

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \caption{Validation Cases: CTM vs COMSOL Comparison}
  \label{tab:validation_cases}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}llccrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    \textbf{ID} & \textbf{$\theta$ ($^\circ$)} & \textbf{$t_{\text{TEC}}$ ($\mu$m)} & \textbf{$I$ (mA)} & \textbf{$q$ (W/m²)} & \textbf{CTM ($^\circ$C)} & \textbf{Error (\%)} \\
    \midrule
    1 & 30 & 100 & 50 & 500 & 36.04 & -43.2 \\
    2 & 30 & 150 & 100 & 500 & 31.81 & -36.4 \\
    3 & 30 & 150 & 80 & 500 & 30.68 & -33.8 \\
    4 & 30 & 200 & 120 & 2000 & 58.23 & -61.5 \\
    5 & 45 & 150 & 80 & 1000 & 44.85 & -52.4 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The validation reveals that the CTM systematically overpredicts the maximum temperature compared to the FEM solution (negative error implies COMSOL predicted a lower temperature, indicating better actual cooling than the CTM suggests). This overprediction is rooted in the fundamental 1D radial assumption of the model. In a 3D geometry, heat inherently spreads azimuthally around the gaps and through the silicon layer in complex patterns. When these multidimensional spreading paths are mathematically reduced to a discrete 1D string of nodes, the model artificially concentrates the thermal bottlenecks, leading to elevated temperature predictions.

While an overpredicting thermal model is conservative and generally preferred for safe engineering design, the magnitude of the error dictates that the CTM cannot be universally applied blindly. Therefore, characterising the strict validity domain wherein the 1D assumption holds is critical for deploying this tool in parametric design optimization.

